; A source program is normally saved as `main.u`. Start with a main function.
; In this repair, an explicit main must contain exactly one `kill` statement.

  void main() <void> {
      io.out("Hello, Underscore!")
      kill -silent
  }

; `void` means the function returns no value. `main` is the program entry point.
; `io.out(value)` prints one value and a newline. `kill -silent` exits successfully.

..2. comments
; A semicolon starts a line comment. A `;- ... -;` pair surrounds a block comment.

  ; This comment ends at the end of this line.

  ;- A longer comment can span
     several lines. -;

..3. standard-library imports
; The library root and `with` module list must be on separate lines.
; A comma-separated module list is valid, but it belongs on the `with` line.

  ?std
      with io, math

  void main() <void> {
      fp root = math.sqrt(49.0)
      io.out(root)
      kill -silent
  }

; Importing `io` makes the API dependency clear. In this compiler snapshot,
; `io.out` and `io.in` are native C-backend intrinsics; `io._` documents that
; contract and `std.ufg` lists signatures. They are not ordinary overloaded
; function bodies in `io._` yet.

; Do not write `?std with io, math` on one line: the compiler now rejects it.

..4. variables and primitive types
; `int` is an integer, `fp` is floating point, and `str` is text.
; A variable can be initialized when declared. `void` is a function return type.

  int count = 12
  fp ratio = 0.75
  str title[24] = "Chapter One"
  const int limit = 100

; `[N]` sets a writable string-buffer capacity. `const` declarations need an
; initializer. Avoid assigning into a constant after its declaration.

..5. arithmetic and assignment
; Arithmetic: + - * / %. Updates: ++ --. Compound assignments include += -= *=
; /= %= &= |= ^=. Parentheses can make evaluation order more obvious.

  int total = 2 + 3 * 4
  total += 2
  total++
  io.out(total)

..6. comparisons and conditions
; Numeric comparisons: == != < > <= >=. Logical operators include &&, || and !.
; `elif` is an additional branch; `else` is the fallback.

  if (total >= 10 && !false) {
      io.out("ten or more")
  } elif (total > 0) {
      io.out("positive")
  } else {
      io.out("zero or less")
  }

..7. functions and returns
; A function starts with a return type, then a name and typed parameters.
; `retreat value` returns a value. Bare `retreat` exits a void function.
; Function flags currently supported by this patch are `void`, `static`, `inline`.
; `void` is the existing process/signature marker; `static` requests internal C
; linkage; `inline` emits an internal-linkage C inline helper.

  int add(int left, int right) <void> {
      retreat left + right
  }

  int twice(int value) <static,inline> {
      retreat value * 2
  }

  void main() <void> {
      io.out(add(3, twice(4)))
      kill -silent
  }

; Other function flags are rejected until their behavior is defined and emitted.
; The `main` entry point may use `<void>` but not `<static>` or `<inline>`.

..8. fixed-width types
; The lexer recognizes int8/int16/int32/int64, fp8/fp16/fp32/fp64 and
; str8/str16/str32/str64. Fixed-width declarations use `type@name`.

  int32@packetCount = 100
  fp64@precision = 0.000001
  io.out(packetCount)
  io.out(precision)

; The integer widths map to C fixed-width integer types. fp64 maps to double.
; fp8/fp16 and narrow string widths still depend on backend representation.

..9. arrays and string buffers
; `<N>` declares an array element count; the initializer uses square brackets.
; A string matrix can combine a per-row text capacity `[N]` and row count `<N>`.

  int scores<3> = [10, 20, 30]
  io.out(scores[1])
  str names[12]<2> = ["Ada", "Lin"]
  io.out(names[0])

; Initializer count and element types are checked. Prefer arrays over `vec`:
; dynamic vectors are not implemented by the current runtime.

..10. pointers
; `->` declares a single pointer. `@value` takes an address and `<-pointer`
; dereferences it. `-->` and `<--pointer` express a double pointer.

  int score = 20
  int ->pScore = @score
  io.out(<-pScore)

  int ->firstPointer = @score
  int -->secondPointer = @firstPointer
  io.out(<--secondPointer)

; Respect pointer depth and lifetime. Do not return the address of a local value.

..11. loops
; `during` repeats while a condition is true. `repeat` is a counted loop.
; `cont` skips to the next iteration; `break` exits the loop or switch.

  int count = 0
  during (count < 3) {
      io.out(count)
      count++
  }

  repeat (int index = 0, index < 3, index++) {
      io.out(index)
  }

..12. switch
; Use `arg` for a case and `incase` for the default. Only one default branch is
; allowed. The current C backend requires an integer-like switch expression.

  switch (count) {
      arg 1:
          io.out("one")
          break
      incase:
          io.out("other")
          break
  }

..13. package manifests
; `&folder` loads the package manifest at `folder/folder.ufg` relative to the
; entry source file, then loads modules listed under `?files` from that folder.
; Use this shorthand for project packages; legacy `?manifest folder` is rejected.

  &logs

; The folder should contain `logs.ufg` and the listed `._` module files (with
; no space in the actual `._` suffix). See packaging.md for a full layout.

..14. program termination
; When an explicit `main` exists, use exactly one `kill` in the full program.
; The compiler rejects a second main, multiple kill statements, or a main without
; one kill. Place the single kill at the end of main after resource cleanup.

  void main() <void> {
      io.out("Finished")
      kill -silent
  }

..15. current implementation boundary
; Recognition by the lexer is not a promise of a working runtime implementation.
; `attempt/incase`, classes, and dynamic vectors are not complete C-backend features.
; Native I/O is implemented in the C code-generation/runtime path. The functions
; advertised in std.ufg should be checked against the runtime/codegen before use.
